{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on exact head af89862b1dfbd05b2fc46be8b28628d5fc59c977, reviewed against main 25a7b83785b27ee98f17579e5a8fb238597b31d7. All fourteen effective artifacts inspected and their complete source/report/test delta read; the prepared review binding names implementation request #9475 (78f0971c) and reporting artifact #9794 (4ff14045).\n\n[P2] Recheck cancellation after the repository lookup before sending each revocation — packages/git/src/landing/engine.ts:111-112 at this head.\npublicationTokens.revoke checks over only before await o.repo(). If that lookup stays pending past the shared wait, the wrapper rejects and sets over=true, but the late lookup then calls revokeToken anyway. The same gap affects a retry that entered its lookup before the deadline. An abandoned attempt can therefore send provider work outside its bounded pass and overlap later durable retries. Check the cancellation/deadline immediately after every awaited lookup and immediately before the actual revoke send; retain the known-token debt for a later bounded pass. Add controls for both the initial lookup and a retry lookup held past the deadline. My independent 25-ms deferred-lookup controls both failed: one provider call where zero were expected, and two where one was expected. The existing bounded test holds revokeToken itself, so it misses this gap. Both controls passed when only a post-lookup cancellation check was added in a separate disposable clone; that source patch was then restored. This is one defect with two reproductions, not two findings.\n\nArchitecture: the affected Artroom services are the Git landing driver/storage, canonical mint adapter, and Room SQLite/alarm coordinator. The head implements the adopted R-MINT contract without changing the normative protocol, and changes none of Gitseq's ordinary-Git storage, semantic-free kernel, host or Workroom interpreter contracts. Main 25a7b837 is an ancestor; mints.ts and docs/protocol.md are byte-identical to it. The atomic pushToken transaction records the attempt ID and keyed landing row and claims the ledger record together; tokenRevoked removes its own row with its owner. The one-time fill covers active and ended cleanup owners, and knownToken uses a point lookup. The Room constructs one ledger per object start, stores wake before mint sends, includes nextDue in recovery/alarm composition, runs mints last and gives it its own self-timed failure fence. The existing idle-write fences and gone-repository retention remain part of the combined candidate.\n\nSecurity: publication mint ownership and 60-second write scope remain explicit. Mint failure details use allow-listed safe metadata rather than provider messages; uncertain creates are retained, never attributed by an inventory or swept, and known revocations use their own IDs. The finding above leaves the revocation execution boundary incomplete. The authority chain was inspected: planner's effective operator foundation #1, explicit contract/lane commissioning request #8651, independent ratified design approval #9463, sealed landing #9465 satisfying that commission, and effective unretired non-stale merged design artifact #9467; lane B's request explicitly requires that adopted design and separate review, and builder's effective promise #9706 began after lane A landed. This authorizes source work, not live credentials or deployment. The three pre-existing landing provider-text sinks are still open under effective separate request #9796 (d29c09fa), which I inspected; this review does not claim they were repaired.\n\nSimplification: retaining the existing landing owner and adding a keyed token projection permits the shared ledger to replace duplicated canonical mint/retry behavior without an operation-history scan. canonicalTokens, including its unused read mint, has no remaining TypeScript caller. The mints fence reuses the fixed loop mechanism; pending unknown records do not cause a five-second loop. Repair the awaited-lookup boundary in the current bounded revocation path and keep the distinct late-create settlement semantics. No additional simplification defect was found.\n\nThe report's carried choices do not establish further defects: recovery after the stopped-host fault point or a double storage failure may require a new object start; rolled-back pushToken releases off the publication queue; landed cleanup rows keep plan 003's revocation debt past expiry; gone repositories retain ledger records while work pauses; and a last already-stored timeout wake may run once after a backlog drains. The tests exercise the 45-record held-revoke backlog, concurrent successful publication, stored post-wake lifetime, earlier unrelated alarms, future observation timing, restart ownership, unknown retention, idle zero-write behavior and durable mints fences. Lane C and the operator cleanup projection remain separately commissioned work; full cost accounting and the complete platform scope stay open.\n\nValidation: independent clone /tmp/artroom-checker-mint-b-v1. Dependency installation completed; root typecheck and root npm test exited 0 (2,248 passed, 1 skipped, including Git Node 282, Room Node 197 and Room workerd 467). Git Workers exited 0, 11 passed. Room Wrangler dry-run bundling and independent Git harness typecheck exited 0. Expected intentional restart/request-failure diagnostics in workerd did not fail the framework gates. Independently replayed all 32 builder guard mutations in the isolated guards clone, using the affected Node landing or Room workerd suite, plus one checker mutation removing cleanupDone's wait for off-queue releases: all 33 caused named runtime test failures, with zero compile/load failures and zero survivors. Every temporary mutation was restored in finally.\nTwo additional checker timeout controls failed at the candidate and passed only in the disposable patched controls clone; no candidate or canonical source was modified. After all mutations were 